# React/TypeScript Frontend Patterns

Follow React best practices and TypeScript patterns for the frontend application.

## Project Architecture
The React application uses:
- [frontend/src/App.tsx](mdc:frontend/src/App.tsx) - Main application component
- [frontend/src/components/](mdc:frontend/src/components/) - Reusable UI components
- [frontend/src/services/](mdc:frontend/src/services/) - API service layer
- [frontend/src/hooks/](mdc:frontend/src/hooks/) - Custom React hooks
- [frontend/src/types/](mdc:frontend/src/types/) - TypeScript type definitions
- [frontend/src/store/](mdc:frontend/src/store/) - State management (Zustand)
- [frontend/src/contexts/](mdc:frontend/src/contexts/) - React contexts

## State Management
- Use Zustand for global state management
- Use React Query for server state management
- Use local state for component-specific data
- Use React Context sparingly, prefer Zustand for shared state

## Component Patterns
- Use functional components with hooks
- Use TypeScript interfaces for props
- Destructure props at the top of components
- Use proper prop validation and default values
- Keep components focused and single-purpose

## Example Component
```typescript
import React from 'react';
import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';
import { User } from '../types/user';

interface UserListProps {
  onUserSelect: (user: User) => void;
  className?: string;
}

const UserList: React.FC<UserListProps> = ({ onUserSelect, className = '' }) => {
  const { data: users, isLoading, error } = useQuery({
    queryKey: ['users'],
    queryFn: fetchUsers,
  });

  if (isLoading) return <div>Loading...</div>;
  if (error) return <div>Error loading users</div>;

  return (
    <div className={`user-list ${className}`}>
      {users?.map((user) => (
        <UserCard key={user.id} user={user} onSelect={onUserSelect} />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
};
```

## API Service Pattern
- Use Axios for HTTP requests
- Create typed service functions
- Use React Query for caching and state management
- Handle errors gracefully with proper error boundaries

## Custom Hooks
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks
- Use descriptive hook names starting with 'use'
- Return objects with clear property names
- Handle loading and error states
